    <!-- System page -->
    <sys:String x:Key="S.System.OfficeDesc">The coolest and quietest: light work and battery. Slower under heavy work.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.System.BalancedDesc">Everyday use: Windows' balanced settings with the quiet fans.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.System.GamingDesc">For games: quick under load, reasonable at idle; the fans speed up sooner.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.System.PerformanceDesc">The highest speed: the most eager processor, the fans at their top. The hottest and loudest.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.System.Balanced">Balanced (Office)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Lighting.ProfBalanced">Balanced (Office)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.FirstChanges.Creating">Making a restore point before Nextcalibur's first changes to this computer...</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.FirstChanges.NoPointWhy">Windows could not make a restore point before Nextcalibur's first changes (power plan repairs, the mode profile): {0}. They would be made with no way back.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Settings.UsbSavingTitle">USB power saving</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Settings.UsbSavingDesc">On: unused USB devices sleep to save power. Off: they never sleep, so mouse and keyboard receivers never lag. Starts as Windows has it.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Settings.WifiSavingTitle">Wi-Fi power saving</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Settings.WifiSavingDesc">On: the Wi-Fi adapter saves power, mostly on battery. Off: always at full speed, the connection never stalls. Starts as Windows has it.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Settings.SavingMixed">Different in the modes: {0}</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Settings.SavingOn">on</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Settings.SavingOff">off</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Settings.GroupCeilings">Processor ceilings</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Settings.CeilingsTitle">Each mode's ceiling</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Settings.CeilingsDesc">The highest speed each mode may run the processor at, never above the measured limit. Under 100% in Power Saver turns the boost off. Lower it to keep a dusty machine cooler.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Ceiling.Percent">{0}%</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Ceiling.PercentTop">{0}% (highest)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Ceiling.NoLimit">No limit</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Ceiling.Ghz">{0} GHz</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Tour.Step.CeilingRows.Title">Processor ceilings</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Tour.Step.CeilingRows.Body">Each mode's top speed. Power Saver as a percentage, the others as a clock speed with the boost kept on. What you choose is written into the mode's power plan at once and can never go above the upper bound Nextcalibur measured. "Test" finds the highest ceiling that keeps the mode at its temperature.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Plans.ForeignTitle">Another power plan was chosen</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Plans.ForeignBody">{0} is active now, which is none of Nextcalibur's modes. What Nextcalibur does by itself on the modes' plans (the mode on battery, program rules, Emergency Power Saver) has stopped until you choose a mode.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Plans.ChangedTitle">Your power plans were changed outside Nextcalibur</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Plans.ChangedBody">Values Nextcalibur set in {0}'s plan were changed from Windows or another program. Unless you put them back, they stay as they are.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Plans.ChangedAboveBody">Values Nextcalibur set in {0}'s plan were changed, and the ceiling was raised above the measured upper bound. The machine runs hot at that ceiling - putting them back is recommended.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Plans.PutBack">Put Nextcalibur's values back</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.CeilingTest.Button">Test</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.CeilingTest.ButtonTip">Measure this mode's ceiling: at full load, finds the highest ceiling that holds the mode's temperature. What runs in the background is closed first.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.CeilingTest.Title">Ceiling test - {0}</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.CeilingTest.Intro">The processor will run at full load for {0}, and from the ceiling in force down, the highest ceiling that keeps the package under {1} °C is looked for. So that the measurement is right, everything running in the background - open programs, background processes, services that are not Windows', downloads in progress - is closed. Windows itself, security software and drivers are not touched. Services are started again when the test ends; opening the closed programs again is up to you.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.CeilingTest.Blocked">The test cannot start - work that must not be interrupted is going on: {0}. Try again when it has finished. Nothing was closed.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.CeilingTest.Group.Transfer">Downloads and transfers in progress</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.CeilingTest.Group.App">Open programs</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.CeilingTest.Group.Background">Background processes</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.CeilingTest.Group.Service">Services to stop (started again after the test)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.CeilingTest.NothingRunning">Nothing to close.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.CeilingTest.Consent">I have saved my work. I agree that what is left open is closed by force; unsaved work may be lost, and that is my responsibility.</sys:String>
