    <sys:String x:Key="S.Tour.Step.IdleRows.Title">Sleep and screen</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Tour.Step.IdleRows.Body">When the display turns off and the computer sleeps, one row for each mode, plugged in and on battery. Each row is that mode's power plan: the same place as the plan settings in the Control Panel. A change in one shows in the other; nothing changes unless you choose it.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Settings.AskGameTitle">Ask after a game played in a quiet mode</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Settings.AskGameDesc">If a full-screen program ran a few minutes in a quiet mode, you are asked after it closes whether it should use Gaming from now on. Nothing is shown during the game.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Game.AskTitle">{0} was open in {1}</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Game.AskBody">{0} was just covering the screen, and you were in {1}. Switch to Gaming whenever {0} opens from now on? Your mode comes back when it closes.

Answer "No" and this program is not asked about again. The rule can be changed or removed at any time in Settings &gt; Program rules.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Game.AskPerformance">Performance instead of Gaming</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Game.SummaryTitle">While {0} was open</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Tour.Step.SettingAskGameMode.Title">When a game was played in a quiet mode</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Tour.Step.SettingAskGameMode.Body">No need to add games one by one. When a program that covers the screen stayed open in Power Saver or Balanced (Office) for a few minutes, you are asked here after it closes, and the answer becomes a rule. Nothing comes as a notification during a game: heat and suggestions from that time are shown as a summary in this window once it has closed.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Settings.GroupRules">Program rules</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Settings.RulesTitle">A mode for a program</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Settings.RulesDesc">While a program on the list runs, its mode is on; when it closes, your mode returns. A mode you pick by hand meanwhile wins.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Rules.Title">Program rule</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Rules.Started">{0} is running: {1} mode until it closes.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Rules.Ended">{0} closed: back to {1} mode.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Rules.RowActive">{0}  (running now)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Rules.Add">Add</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Rules.Remove">Remove this rule</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Rules.NameHint">A running program from the list, or its name as Task Manager's Details tab shows it, without ".exe"</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Rules.BadName">That is not a program name. Pick one from the list, or type the name as Task Manager's Details tab shows it, without ".exe".</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Tour.Step.SettingProgramRules.Title">A mode for a program</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Tour.Step.SettingProgramRules.Body">Off until you switch it on. Add a program and a mode - pick it from the programs running, or type its name - and while it runs that mode is on; when it closes, the mode you had comes back. Windows says when a program starts or stops; nothing is polled. A mode you choose by hand meanwhile wins.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Settings.FnShortcutsTitle">Shortcuts for the Fn keys</sys:String>
